# a test run locks Object.prototype by its names, and the files of the tests have opened two on it. A store under a key that nobody can read may name one of them, and then nothing throws
# so the build says nothing of it to a file of the tests: where the store is refused the run says so, at the test and at its end
# a file that an executable holds too is told that the name cannot be read: what the tests have opened is open for it too, so not that the store throws if it runs
# a.test.js: import "./src/polyfill.js"; function lay(name) { Object.prototype[name] = () => 7; delete Object.prototype[name]; }   src/polyfill.js, of the product: the same function
# locked-writes/a-name-that-nobody-reads-on-an-object-that-is-locked-whole.ir is the control: the same store in an executable
# OPTIONS: admit=ReplacedBuiltins
# DIAGNOSTIC: LockedBuiltinWriteWithUnknownKey 2
# RUN: locks

